  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CARDIO-INDIA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Heart disease and strokes (cardiovascular disease) cause most deaths in India.  Older people often have extra risk due to their age, not getting enough physical activity, being overweight, or having high blood pressure or diabetes. They also often have problems accessing care and complying with treatment.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Older adults (aged 60 years and over) accessing Mobile Health Units, who have high blood pressure and may or may not be taking antihypertensive treatment, . The research will also benefit their caregivers, community health workers and primary healthcare providers involved in their care.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Our aim is to improve the early detection, treatment and management of metabolic risk factors for Cardiovascular disease among older people in India by co-creating, implementing and evaluating a community and primary health system-based intervention (CARDIO-INDIA).  OBJECTIVES: Undertake initial research to understand the main challenges of detection, treatment and management of metabolic risk factors. To co-create, implement and evaluate CARDIO-INDIA&#x2019;s effectiveness and cost-effectiveness in improving patient-related clinical outcomes versus usual care [Work Package (WP) 1 and 2]. Understand acceptability, barriers, facilitators, and uptake to implementing CARDIO-INDIA at an individual, healthcare provider, and health system level (WP3). Develop a toolkit to enable scale up of CARDIO-INDIA (if found effective) across India and other Low and Middle Income Countries (WP4)</narrative>
  </description>
